55-45 100-100 100z m-400-550c-138 0-250 112-250 250 0 138 112 250 250 250 138 0 250-112 250-250 0-138-112-250-250-250z m365 380c-19 0-35-16-35-35 0-19 16-35 35-35 19 0 35 16 35 35 0 19-16 35-35 35z" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 850)"></path></svg></a><a rel="tooltip" class="modebar-btn" data-title="Open in dialog (edit layout)" data-toggle="false" data-gravity="n"><svg viewBox="0 0 1000 1000" class="icon" height="1em" width="1em"><path d="M786 296v-267q0-15-11-26t-25-10h-214v214h-143v-214h-214q-15 0-25 10t-11 26v267q0 1 0 2t0 2l321 264 321-264q1-1 1-4z m124 39l-34-41q-5-5-12-6h-2q-7 0-12 3l-386 322-386-322q-7-4-13-4-7 2-12 7l-35 41q-4 5-3 13t6 12l401 334q18 15 42 15t43-15l136-114v109q0 8 5 13t13 5h107q8 0 13-5t5-13v-227l122-102q5-5 6-12t-4-13z" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 850)"></path></svg></a></div></div></div><svg class="main-svg" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="670.6666666666667" height="282.33333333333337"><g class="hoverlayer"></g></svg></div></div></div></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(660,20)" x="660" y="20" title="codeHTML" width="560" height="323" class="codeHTML" name="codeHTML" rot="0"><foreignObject x="0" y="0" width="560" height="323" style="width: 560px; height: 323px;"><div name="htmlCode" style="zoom: 0.56; height: 576.786px; width: 1000px;" code="&lt;div class=&quot;ch&quot;&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;Colpitts oscillator&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;div class=&quot;ch-card&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;How it works&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The tank circuit is the inductor &lt;strong&gt;L1&lt;/strong&gt; with the capacitive divider &lt;strong&gt;C1 / C2&lt;/strong&gt;. The &lt;strong&gt;midpoint of the divider is grounded&lt;/strong&gt;: the collector &lt;code&gt;a&lt;/code&gt; and the base side &lt;code&gt;k&lt;/code&gt; are therefore in opposite phase and the feedback is positive.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The feedback ratio is set by &lt;code&gt;C1 / C2&lt;/code&gt;. The oscillation grows until the transistor limits the amplitude.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;RFC&lt;/strong&gt; (1 mH) feeds the collector from the supply and blocks the AC signal. &lt;strong&gt;Cb&lt;/strong&gt; couples the tank to the base.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;Frequency&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;f = 1 / (2&amp;pi; &amp;radic;(L1 &amp;middot; C1 &amp;middot; C2 / (C1 + C2)))&lt;/code&gt; = 1 / (2&amp;pi; &amp;radic;(100 &amp;micro;H &amp;middot; 1 nF)) &amp;asymp; 503 kHz&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;In the PyAMS simulation: about 510 kHz.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;Circuit&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;V1&lt;/strong&gt; = 12 V. &lt;strong&gt;Q1&lt;/strong&gt;: model &lt;code&gt;BJT&lt;/code&gt; (NPN). &lt;strong&gt;R1&lt;/strong&gt; = 47 k&amp;Omega; and &lt;strong&gt;R2&lt;/strong&gt; = 10 k&amp;Omega; bias the base, &lt;strong&gt;RE&lt;/strong&gt; = 470 &amp;Omega; limits the gain (and the amplitude).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;L1&lt;/strong&gt; = 100 &amp;micro;H, &lt;strong&gt;C1&lt;/strong&gt; = &lt;strong&gt;C2&lt;/strong&gt; = 2 nF, &lt;strong&gt;Cb&lt;/strong&gt; = 10 nF.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;L1 has an initial current&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;Ic&lt;/code&gt; = 10 mA): it starts the oscillation, as the noise does in a real circuit.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Time domain analysis: 0 ... 200 &amp;micro;s with a step of 20 ns. Plotted: the collector &lt;code&gt;a&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;k&lt;/code&gt;. The amplitude settles after about 100 &amp;micro;s.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The picture of the oscillator is simplified: the bias network, the RFC and the emitter resistor were added to get a working circuit.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
"> 
